","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔS = Σn * ΔS°f(products) - Σn * ΔS°f(reactants)

where n is the number of moles and ΔS°f is the standard molar entropy of the substance.

For the reaction:

2 CO(g) + O2(g) → 2 CO2(g)

The standard molar entropies (S°f) at 298 K are:

S°f(CO) = 197.7 J/(mol·K)
S°f(O2) = 205.1 J/(mol·K)
S°f(CO2) = 213.7 J/(mol·K)

Now, we can plug these values into the equation:

ΔS = [2 * S°f(CO2)] - [2 * S°f(CO) + S°f(O2)]

ΔS = [2 * 213.7 J/(mol·K)] - [2 * 197.7 J/(mol·K) + 205.1 J/(mol·K)]

ΔS = [427.4 J/(mol·K)] - [399.4 J/(mol·K)]

ΔS = 28.0 J/(mol·K)

Since we have 3 moles of CO, we need to multiply the entropy change by 3:

ΔS = 28.0 J/(mol·K) * 3

ΔS = 84.0 J/K

The change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction is 84.0 J/K.
