1. The solid benzoic acid dissolves in the solution.
2. The solution becomes basic (pH > 7), as sodium hydroxide is a strong base.
3. The solution may also be warmed due to the heat released during the reaction.

The fully ionized equation for the reaction is:
\[
\text{H(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2\text{)} + \text{NaOH} \rightarrow \text{Na(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2\text{)} + \text{H}_2\text{O}
\]
Here, benzoic acid donates a proton (H⁺) to sodium hydroxide, forming sodium benzoate (Na(C₇H₅O₂)) and water (H₂O). The products are sodium benzoate, a water-soluble salt, and water. 

Final answer: $\boxed{\text{H(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2\text{)} + \text{NaOH} \rightarrow \text{Na(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2\text{)} + \text{H}_2\text{O}}$

