","In probability theory, a probability law is a rule that describes the probability of distinct outcomes in a experiment. It is defined as a function $\mathbb{P}$ that maps elements of the sample space (all possible outcomes) to probabilities, which are nonnegative numbers that sum to 1. Mathematically, it is defined as:
\[
\mathbb{P} \in \begin{bmatrix} \begin{array}{aligned}
&\text{A} \in \mathcal{S} \\
&\text{A} \text{ is a integerable element of the sample space } \mathcal{S} \\
&\text{The probability of } A \text{ is } \mathbb{P}(A) \geq 0 \\
&\text{The probability of the entire sample space is } \mathbb{P}(\mathcal{S}) = 1
\end{array}
\end{bmatrix}
\]
This rule is also known as a probability function or probability density function (PDF) in some cases.
