","To determine the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a photon scattered by an electron, we use the Compton effect formula:

\[
\lambda_f - \lambda_i = \frac{2\pi\hbar}{m_e c} (1 - \cos \theta)
\]

where:
- \(\lambda_f = 2.00 \, \text{Å}\) (final wavelength),
- \(\lambda_i = 2.00 \, \text{Å}\) (initial wavelength),
- \(\hbar = 1.0545 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J·s}\) (reduced Planck constant),
- \(m_e = 9.109 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}\) (electron mass),
- \(c = 2.998 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}\) (speed of light),
- \(\theta = 180^\circ\) (maximum wavelength shift occurs when \(\theta = 180^\circ\), i.e., the electron recolides back back back).

Substituting \(\theta = 180^\circ\) into the Compton effect formula:

\[
\lambda_f - 2.00 \, \text{Å} = \frac{2\pi \times 1.0545 \times 10^{-34}}{9.109 \times 10^{-31} \times 2.998 \times 10^8} (1 - \cos 180^\circ)
\]

\[
\lambda_f - 2.00 \, \text{Å} = \frac{2\pi \times 1.0545 \times 10^{-34}}{9.109 \times 10^{-31} \times 2.998 \times 10^8} (2)
\]

\[
\lambda_f - 2.00 \, \text{Å} = 2.24 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{m}
\]

\[
\lambda_f = 2.00 \, \text{Å} + 2.24 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{m} = 2.00 \, \text{Å} + 0.224 \, \text{Å} = 2.224 \, \text{Å}
\]

The maximum percentage change is:

\[
\frac{\lambda_f - \lambda_i}{\lambda_i} \times 100 = \frac{2.224 - 2.00}{2.00} \times 100 = 11.12\%
\]

Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of the photon is \(\boxed{11.12\%}\).
