What is the net chemical reaction that occurs during the discharging of a lead storage battery?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The net chemical reaction for the discharge of a lead storage battery is:
\[ \text{Pb(s)} + \text{PbO}_2(\text{s}) + 4\text{H}^+(aq) + 2\text{SO}_4^{2-}(aq) \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + 2\text{H}_2\text{O(l)} \]

**Explanation:**
1. **Oxidation at the Anode (Anode Reaction):**
   \[ \text{Pb(s)} + \text{H}_2\text{SO}_4(aq) \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + \text{H}^+(aq) \]
   - Lead (Pb) is oxidized to lead sulfate (PbSO₄).

2. **Reduction at the Cathode (Cathode Reaction):**
   \[ \text{PbO}_2(\text{s}) + 2\text{H}^+(aq) + 2\text{SO}_4^{2-}(aq) + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + \text{H}_2\text{O(l)} \]
   - Lead dioxide (PbO₂) is reduced to lead sulfate (PbSO₄).

3. **Net Reaction:**
   - The two half-reactions are combined, and the electrons are canceled out, yielding the net reaction:
   \[ \text{Pb(s)} + \text{PbO}_2(\text{s}) + 4\text{H}^+(aq) + 2\text{SO}_4^{2-}(aq) \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + 2\text{H}_2\text{O(l)} \]

Thus, during discharge, the lead and lead dioxide electrodes are reduced to lead sulfate, and water is formed. The electrolyte (sulfuric acid) is consumed during the process.
