","(a) The force on the proton is given by \(F = qE\), where \(q\) is the charge of the proton and \(E\) is the electric field strength.  
\[
F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}) \times (500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}
\]
The acceleration of the proton is then:  
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.85 \times 10^7 \, \text{m/s}^2
\]  
Thus, the magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{4.85 \times 10^7 \, \text{m/s}^2}\).  

(b) The time \(t\) required for the proton to reach a speed \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\) is given by:  
\[
v = u + at
\]  
where \(u = 0 \, \text{m/s}\) is the initial speed.  
\[
t = \frac{v - u}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s} - 0 \, \text{m/s}}{4.85 \times 10^7 \, \text{m/s}^2} = 6.31 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{s}
\]  
Thus, the time required is \(\boxed{6.31 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{s}}\).
