A = π(r²) = π(0.05 cm)² = 0.00785 cm²

2. Calculate the flow velocity:
v = Q / A = 10 cm³ min⁻¹ / 0.00785 cm² = 1370 cm min⁻¹

3. Calculate the residence time for different lengths of the flow tube:
t = L / v

For a 1 cm long tube:
t = 1 cm / 1370 cm min⁻¹ = 0.00073 s ≈ 0.73 ms

For a 2 cm long tube:
t = 2 cm / 1370 cm min⁻¹ = 0.00146 s ≈ 1.46 ms

For a 3 cm long tube:
t = 3 cm / 1370 cm min⁻¹ = 0.00215 s ≈ 2.15 ms

And so on for the remaining lengths (7 cm, 8 cm, 9 cm, 10 cm).

The reaction times that can be followed depend on the rate of the reaction. If the reaction is very fast, the time it takes for the reaction to proceed will be much shorter than the residence time of the fluid in the tube. In this case, the reaction time will be determined by the rate of the reaction, and the flow tube length can be chosen to minimize the residence time to minimize the time required for the reaction to proceed.

If the reaction is relatively slow, the flow tube length can be chosen to allow the reaction to proceed to completion during the residence time. In this case, the reaction time will be equal to the residence time of the fluid in the tube.
