Gravitational waves have a minus to less one-tenth of a second effect on the trajectory and stability of a spacecraft in orbit around a planet. This effect is negligible for most practical space applications. The gravitational waves are too very small to have any significant influence on the orbit of a spacecraft. The effects of gravitational waves are strongest when the spacecraft is located near a massive celestial body, such as a asteroid or a galaxy, and even in these cases, the effect is typically negligible. Thus, measures to mitigate their effects are not required for space travel.

